92
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:56,560
Stop throwing good money after bad and I just wrote off a hundred thousand dollars that I'd paid out.

93
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:00,400
That's when I started thinking, okay, I need to work with someone who's got a little bit more skin in the game.

94
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:01,200
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

95
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:02,480
Developers without downside risk.

96
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,920
There's nothing worse in the world.

97
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:04,480
Yeah.

98
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,360
But then that has its own challenges.

99
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:10,880
When someone's working on it as a side project, they can get busy with their jobs.

100
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,440
And so it's been a struggle to work with developers that model.

101
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:25,520
So I don't want to spend too much time on the history, but I think one other thing that is probably related to the 3.0 launch for WeatherUp is what was your marketing strategy for all these apps then?

102
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:30,160
You presumably didn't have a huge advertising budget or something, at least initially.

103
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:33,280
How has that kind of evolved over time with all your apps?

104
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:34,720
Yeah, it depends on the app.

105
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:37,440
I've relied very heavily on organic channels.

106
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:38,960
I've done very little paid advertising.

107
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:43,360
Early in the App Store, I actually did spend thousands of dollars trying to make it work.

108
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:46,720
And like with paid apps, it was just a total money losing proposition.

109
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:55,120
But mostly I've relied on getting attention., Apple's attention, press attention, and then ASO has been really big for my apps generally.

110
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,080
Like Launch Center Pro was the top result for a bunch of like Launcher and other kinds of terms.

111
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:02,160
So it got a lot of browse traffic.

112
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:07,680
That's actually one of the things that we'll get to with a weather app, trying to get any kind of organic traffic.

113
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,360
Those keywords are just so crowded, which makes it really hard.

114
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:14,160
But one of my most successful apps was a mirror app.

115
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:21,280
And go figure, at its peak something like ten or twenty thousand people searched the app store for mirror every day.

116
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:24,560
And I was capturing six, eight thousand of those.

117
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:25,600
8,000 of those every single day.

118
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,320
As an ignorant person, what does a mirror app provide that like the camera doesn't?

119
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:33,680
So it's genuinely a good app.

120
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,440
And I made an embarrassing mistake.

121
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:36,800
I didn't imply it wasn't, David.

122
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:45,680
One of the main things is that the front facing camera, if you just launch the camera app, it's in a little window with all the chrome around it.

123
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:47,920
So this launches to like a full screen mirror.

124
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:48,880
You can zoom in.

125
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:50,400
We did some really cool stuff.

126
00:08:50,560 --> 00:09:00,560
Like you tap a little flashlight button and it puts white around the edges of the screen and turns the brightness up to 100% so that in dark or weak light situations it's actually.

127
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,680
really helpful, like one of those lighted mirrors and stuff.

128
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,960
And then we had a really cool way where you tap the screen to like freeze the frame and then you can save that as a photo.

129
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:14,000
So it's a kind of cool way to preview your selfie before you save it, little things like that.

130
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:15,040
But people love that app.

131
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,920
I guess it becomes a thing you know to search for, right?

132
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:22,880
It's interesting the different kinds of niches will inform your strategy, like how you can acquire users.

133
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,160
I think it's almost the opposite.

134
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:28,160
I think it's like, I need to check to see if my lipstick looks right.

135
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:31,040
And they don't even think, I can open the camera app.

136
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:32,880
They're just like, is there like a mirror thing?

137
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,240
And then you just search for it.

138
00:09:34,720 --> 00:10:16,320
You're trying to solve a problem and you go to the app store if the app store is your default search engine uh it can drive a surprising amount of creating that loop you know what i mean like convincing people with normal human people problems to look for software let's just think about how magical that is for a moment yeah i'm always surprised when i look at my app figures aso tab and see that i'm ranking well and getting downloads from full sentences like i want a green noise i make a white noise app a green noise for free or something like that and it's like people are just typing full-blown search queries but yeah i ask that because in the earlylier era, getting attention, if that's kind of your big marketing strategy.

139
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:22,320
I don't necessarily want to say it's easier, but it's definitely different because like Apple features in the early years, it was way easier.

140
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:23,680
How many apps are there now, David?

141
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,480
Like, there's millions and millions.

142
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:31,920
There were like when I launched my first app, there were 270 apps on the App Store, you didn't need attention.

143
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:33,920
There were 270 apps on the App Store.

144
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:34,160
Yeah.

145
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:34,480
Yeah.

146
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:38,400
I guess there's always people talk about literally scrolling the list of apps.

147
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:38,800
Yeah.

148
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:40,640
You could look at every single app, right?

149
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:47,600
And by 2011 or 2012, I mean, it was different, but it still we were talking probably an order of magnitude fewer apps than there are today.

150
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:57,040
And it's still to some degree., like getting attention, especially in the early teens, you know, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013 was like the most predictable thing you could do, right?

151
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:58,240
And to some degree it is.

152
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:03,600
And I think it's probably one of the better, more predictable ways to try to break through the noise if you know how to do it.

153
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:04,800
But yeah, we should talk about it.

154
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:07,680
Yeah, yeah, especially if you don't have any marketing budget.

155
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:09,680
So yeah, let's talk about WeatherUp then.

156
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:17,600
And like you mentioned earlier, WeatherUp was a rebrand of Weather Atlas, you said, and there have been multiple versions of it over time.

157
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,520
So when it came time to this WeatherUp 3.0 launch, what was the state of WeatherUp at that time?

158
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:27,200
Well, I mean, weather Up 3.0 has been a long time in the making.

159
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:33,200
As I mentioned, you know, working with App Partner, my cousin, he was busy with other stuff and had a lot going on.

160
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,680
And so this has been a very long, slow.

161
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,960
It started in 2019, actually, like the designs for the widget.

162
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:40,800
That's when Charlie started doing apps.

163
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:41,600
Yeah, actually, yeah.

164
00:11:41,920 --> 00:11:42,000
Yeah.

165
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,560
So when I first opened Xcode for real.

166
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:57,680
What kind of kept happening was we would get deep enough into one specific feature and then just kind of realized, oh, well, to get attention, we really should be doing X and we finished Y.

167
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:00,720
And I mean, at this point, I actually regret not just shipping.

168
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:02,880
So like we were pretty far off with that.

169
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:04,480
Apple Watch app.

170
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:05,600
Yeah, just ship.

171
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:12,480
Like we should have shipped the Apple Watch app in 2020, probably when that was ready, but that was when widgets got really big.

172
00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:14,400
And so we're like, now we got to build a widget.

173
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:19,360
So at WWC 2020, we're like, okay, we got to build a widget.

174
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:22,000
We can't just launch the Apple Watch app.

175
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:27,440
Then we were so close to launching the widget and then Apple announced interactive widgets.

176
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:31,840
And it's like, we can't just launch a widget when everybody else is launching interactive widgets.

177
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:34,880
And so we should have just launched, but we held off.

178
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:36,800
The tough thing about getting attention.

179
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:39,680
is that you do have to do something unique.

180
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:48,800
Well, widgets were really cool and unique in 2020 when they first were homescreen widgets, but 2021, that's not enough to get attention.

181
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:52,640
Apple Watch apps, complications like all these things, and that's a trade-off.

182
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:57,680
The calculus for me was, well, let's wait and try to make a big splash with all these things.

183
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:04,960
But really like at this point, we could have already launched the widget and just adding interaction at the level we did would have gotten attention.

184
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:07,120
You'd be on WeatherUp 7.0.

185
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:08,560
Yeah, exactly.

186
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:15,120
So presumably then that long of an amount of time without major ne new features was impacting the business then?

187
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:17,520
Or were downloads pretty much just swimming along?

188
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:19,520
Yeah, I mean, that's one of the big regrets.

189
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:41,200
I was actually just looking at Appfigures recently and downloads were pretty consistently good and we had some nice spikes from getting attention or being part of big launches with new iOS features and stuff like that all the way up until about 2019 when things went on hiatus and we were working towards this bigger update and things really dropped off the map.

190
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:44,480
Like Apple stopped featuring the app because it hadn't been updated for a while.

191
00:13:44,960 --> 00:13:48,400
We stopped getting any kind of bumps from these new updates and things like that.

192
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:58,400
So coming into the Weather App 3.0, I mean, it's almost like launching a whole new app because downloads were at low double digits a day, like 15 downloads a day.

193
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:04,160
I mean, I guess for some independent apps, that's a lot, but it's hard to build a business and make any money on that.

194
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:05,280
So the revenue was down.

195
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:07,760
I've actually been losing money on the app for like 18 months.

196
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:10,800
I've been funding it out of my investing in the future, David.

197
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,480
Investing in the future, yes.

198
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:14,400
And again, I think that was one of the mistakes.

199
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:26,240
And that's the beauty of subscription apps too, is that except for getting attention, there's no incentive to hold back features like launching a big 3.0 where you're doing a paid update where everyone's paying for the features.

200
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:30,320
Well, nobody was going to pay me any more because I launched those features.

201
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:32,400
So I should have just launched them and kept moving.

202
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:45,600
Yeah, because even a really massive, big, splashy launch amortized over the time between big launches, that time in between big launches is arguably much more important than how splashy the launch itself is.

203
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:51,440
Unless you're just starting out, that's where it can make a really big difference to start from a big stepping stone.

204
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,920
I think the big launch philosophy has been one of the great mistakes of my career.

205
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:03,280
There's so many incentives to be like, oh yeah, we're just going to roll this together into a big thing or like we're going to put this all together in the fall.

206
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:06,400
You're honestly justifying not shipping, right?

