00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:15,520
You had to hold back those features.

252
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:22,880
And early in the App Store, you would release a new SKU as a paid update because there was no way to monetize that user base over time.

253
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:40,000
I think a lot of what you're just saying, I keep thinking, yes, because subscription business model allows you to accrue that value over time and make it compound versus the old model of paid apps and paid updates didn't allow you to do that.

254
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:45,520
Point taken, like, nobody cares about those things anymore, and so don't hold off on shipping.

255
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:55,040
And that was a mistake I made, was for the last four years kind of getting caught in that old mindset that did work in the past for me, but no longer works the same way, because that's not how I run my business.

256
00:17:55,680 --> 00:18:06,400
I think each small update is an opportunity to reach for attention and maybe not like sending an email to your press contact or whatever because that you can oversaturate.

257
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:16,800
But if you're constantly making small updates and each time you can make a little video or something of that update, every single one of those is a chance to catch somebody's news attention.

258
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:21,520
And oftentimes, what they're going to see is an old feature that just happens to be part of your demo.

259
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,040
You're like cashing in on an old feature or something each one of those times.

260
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:30,080
Like most people aren't getting tired of you tweeting too often because most people don't see it in the first place.

261
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:36,560
And then, like you said earlier, one of you two is if you're constantly tweeting to the point of almost being annoying, it also kind of.

262
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,280
signals I get this with RevenueCat stuff.

263
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:43,840
You guys are pushing so much stuff out, I can barely keep up, which kind of shows that I'm on top of things.

264
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:45,600
We're constantly coming out with new stuff.

265
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,800
Even if you don't use it, that signal adds value, perceived value anyway to your product.

266
00:18:51,440 --> 00:19:00,880
And for a weather app like this, I mean, I'm sure a lot of it's the mass market, but for getting a start, for getting anything written about it or whatever, you have to at least appeal to the connoisseur of apps, right?

267
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:07,520
And somebody's going to check how many updates there were and like, so yeah, let's get to the actual launch itself then.

