271
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:30,960
Press and, you know, hoping to get featured by Apple, we'll get into mistakes, but I kind of did the same playbook I have for years was I know if I do something innovative, I can get some attention for that innovation.

272
00:19:31,760 --> 00:19:41,840
And then typically when you do something really innovative with a feature like interactive widgets that Apple has been really pushing heavily and was a big new kind of part of the OS update, you can get attention.

273
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:52,320
So my goal for the launch was to get as much attention as I could from press, from Apple, from others, make some money and then take that and start experimenting with Apple search ads.

274
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:55,360
And then if that went well, start expanding marketing beyond that.

275
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,640
But that's not how things went, which we'll get to.

276
00:19:58,960 --> 00:19:59,040
Yeah.

277
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:01,360
How were you trying to get that attention?

278
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:02,240
I know you.

279
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:03,520
Most people listening know you.

280
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:04,320
You have a lot of context.

281
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:08,480
What was your general strategy to try and get those press articles and Apple features?

282
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:12,400
People say, Oh, well, you know people and that's how you get featured.

283
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:14,240
But I don't think that's totally true.

284
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:14,960
It helps.

285
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:19,920
They will at least scan my email or at least see that I DM them.

286
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:26,160
And maybe I have like a tiny bit of baked in credibility because they know me or have known me for a while or whatever.

287
00:20:26,480 --> 00:20:33,600
But at the end of the day, and I think about this all the time, is that I'm not actually going to capture their attention unless I do something that they care about.

288
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:47,040
And so for me, thinking about reaching out to all my press contacts, I would be embarrassed to send them, whether up three point zero point one update with like one tiny little feature that's not even interesting.

289
00:20:47,520 --> 00:20:54,320
I still feel a ton of pressure to do something that's actually interesting in the market that they're going to be willing to write about.

290
00:20:54,480 --> 00:20:59,040
Because I'm not going to push someone to write about something that's not actually interesting.

291
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:05,440
And that's where for this particular case update, I worked on a video that demonstrated the features.

292
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:15,360
Logically, Jake Moore, who we've had on the podcast a ton and done a ton of stuff with, the CEO of Superwall, has been talking about for years how impactful videos are for paywalls.

293
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:20,800
And with this interactive widget, it felt like it was just something so visual that it needed a video.

294
00:21:21,040 --> 00:21:27,440
So I was working on the video for the paywall primarily, and it turned out so good that it ended up being my press pitch.

295
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:32,400
So instead of hundreds of words, I mean, you just have so little time to capture someone's attention.

296
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:39,680
So the main way I pitched it was like a DM on Twitter and like, hey, I'm working on some really cool interactivity for widgets and weather.

297
00:21:39,920 --> 00:21:41,040
I think this is really interesting.

298
00:21:41,280 --> 00:21:48,000
And I got quite a few people who were like, yeah, I don't really cover apps anymore or, you know, I wouldn't really cover an app.

299
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:52,240
But they saw the video and were like, wow, that looks really cool.

300
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:59,520
So I think whether or not you have a relationship with someone because a lot of the people I pitched, I didn't know and they didn't know me.

301
00:22:00,080 --> 00:22:06,160
But it's all about communicating that value and it being something that's actually worth it to write about.

302
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:08,000
And that video really helped.

303
00:22:08,320 --> 00:22:10,960
Yeah, because it's not just value to the user.

304
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:12,800
It's also interesting.

305
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,600
interest for a story which are slightly different.

306
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:18,480
You have to help them get their story written for the week, right?

307
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:21,920
Like they have a job, they have a quota, they have a number of things you have to produce.

308
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:28,240
And if you want them, help them do their job and find something mutually beneficial for all three parties, right?

309
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:36,880
The reader, the journalist, and you, if I was to say any sort of distillation of how to do PR, it's that, like build something that's mutually beneficial to all parties.

310
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:37,680
And it's hard.

311
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:39,600
You have to find something they're interested in, right?

312
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:42,320
You read about what they do, you learn about, you know, whatever.

313
00:22:42,480 --> 00:22:48,720
Well, I mean, this is February of 2024, which like every tech and otherwise online outlet in journalism.

314
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:51,280
is disappearing right now, like on the daily.

315
00:22:51,440 --> 00:22:56,000
So maybe you're like the last one to get this in before writing about apps just doesn't exist anymore.

316
00:22:56,240 --> 00:23:01,520
If you needed any more signs that this model of launching is no longer, that might be it.

317
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,840
To the same point, it's not just is it interesting, but is it interesting currently?

