410
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:24,000
But there's also the meaning of like making your launch big, like going for just being loud and everything.

411
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:25,920
And you can be loud once a month.

412
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:27,440
I mean, this is the YC advice.

413
00:29:27,520 --> 00:29:29,840
If you have a launch that goes badly, just launch again.

414
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:32,160
The definition of a bad launch is nobody sees it.

415
00:29:32,320 --> 00:29:33,840
So nobody's going to see you do it again, right?

416
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:35,440
RevenueCat did that?

417
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:36,080
I think so.

418
00:29:36,480 --> 00:29:37,760
I mean, like, stage somewhere.

419
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:40,800
So, like, yeah, we posted on Reddit and it kind of got some steam.

420
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:42,080
And then we launched a beta.

421
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:46,720
Like, every time we fundraising, we're like, RevenueCat's launching out of, bro, bro, we make some stuff up.

422
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:48,640
And it's a little bit of a wink and a knot.

423
00:29:48,800 --> 00:29:50,080
Like, we're always doing stuff, right?

424
00:29:50,320 --> 00:29:54,880
Yeah, I guess it's a little bit of showmanship just to, like, put a narrative to the building you've been doing.

425
00:29:55,200 --> 00:29:57,280
And you can kind of do, but I mean, look at Tesla.

426
00:29:57,360 --> 00:30:02,880
Like, every big company juices up stuff they've been working, you know, they dress it up, they push it.

427
00:30:03,040 --> 00:30:11,840
That's what marketing I mean, every Apple keynote starts with a bunch of things that sound like launches that only all the super nerd is like, we've heard this before, right?

428
00:30:12,080 --> 00:30:13,840
But most people watching it are like, this is new.

429
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:14,560
Like, this is okay.

430
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:18,080
You're going to see who are watching, which is probably who it's for.

431
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:20,480
But yeah, I mean, it's the power of narrative control, right?

432
00:30:20,720 --> 00:30:22,240
Like, you can really kind of drive the narrative.

433
00:30:22,400 --> 00:30:24,800
And I was telling you guys before that started podcasts, it's all made up.

434
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:25,440
Everybody has to.

435
00:30:25,600 --> 00:30:26,320
Oh, it's made up.

436
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:28,080
You can just contrast is made up.

437
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:31,760
But yeah, nothing you can do to control for like just dumb luck.

438
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:36,240
And it feels like in this particular case, kind of the mistake.

439
00:30:36,480 --> 00:30:58,880
If, if you were to say that, would be you made a lot right on this one event, and then things that are outside of your control can go wrong, and you gave up on all of the smaller events that could have happened over the last three years or whatever, and put it all into kind of all your quivers behind, or I don't know the phrase, something with a bow and arrow and putting everything behind the one bow or arrow.

440
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:00,080
All my eggs in one bag.

441
00:31:00,400 --> 00:31:00,880
Yeah, yeah.

442
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:04,000
And so that maybe is like the mistake that happened here.

443
00:31:04,240 --> 00:31:07,600
But I guess maybe the more interesting thing then is what now?

444
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:08,480
What are you going to do?

445
00:31:08,560 --> 00:31:09,680
Are you going to do like Jacob said?

446
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:11,040
Are you going to try and relaunch?

447
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:12,240
Short answer, yes.

448
00:31:12,240 --> 00:31:15,200
Yes, I wanted to actually go through more of the failures.

449
00:31:15,360 --> 00:31:15,920
Oh, yeah.

450
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:33,280
And to give the audience a little bit of context, Jacob and I were chatting Friday and I was like, we're talking about the podcast broadly and we've had some really great guests on, but one of the things I want to do more is talk to people who have failed and talk about even those who have succeeded about all the failures leading up to the success.

451
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:36,400
And I was like, yeah, I failed so bad with WeatherApp.

452
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:38,560
And Jacob was like, well, let's talk about it.

453
00:31:39,280 --> 00:31:44,000
If we're going to get other people on the podcast to talk about failures, why don't you lead by example?

454
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:47,680
And so this is Monday, like three days later, and that's exactly what we're doing.

455
00:31:48,080 --> 00:32:00,160
So I actually want to talk through more of the things I think really failed at the launch before we get to how I hope to potentially recover from this failed launch, because I think it's really instructive.

456
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:05,360
And then for me, doing all this stuff, it informs the questions I ask on the podcast.

457
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:07,680
You know, I'm using RevenueCat experiments in the app.

458
00:32:07,920 --> 00:32:11,200
It informs how I talk to my colleagues about our product internally.

459
00:32:11,760 --> 00:32:13,680
I give tons of advice on subclub.

460
00:32:13,760 --> 00:32:15,280
I get tons of advice on subclub.

461
00:32:15,360 --> 00:32:17,360
Apparently, I didn't take all the advice.

462
00:32:18,720 --> 00:32:22,000
I think one of the other big failures I wanted to discuss was pricing.

463
00:32:22,640 --> 00:32:30,480
And specifically because we talk so much about it pricing and I kind of just failed at every level of the typical advice that we give.

464
00:32:31,200 --> 00:32:40,800
So one of them is pricing is not as important as you might think and it's probably better to launch with a lower price and just get more people using the app and get more subscribers.

465
00:32:41,200 --> 00:32:49,040
And I launched with what is probably the highest ticket weather app subscription at $4 a month was fine, but the $40 a year.

466
00:32:49,760 --> 00:32:56,720
But interestingly, my biggest regret is not the $40 a year, it's that I didn't do a sale on launch.

467
00:32:56,960 --> 00:33:07,280
So this is the advice I would have if you are going to do a big attention grabbing thing, do it with some kind of discount, 25% off, 50% off, whatever.

468
00:33:07,520 --> 00:33:13,040
Because all that attention I did get, most of the people who wrote about it listed the price.

469
00:33:13,520 --> 00:33:22,720
A lot of people who maybe even read the TechCrunch article, read it on nine to five, Mac, read it on MacRimors or whatever, Boy Genius Report, they all saw $40 and were like, yeah, no way.

470
00:33:23,120 --> 00:33:28,320
But if they had seen $40, but 50% off, I think we would have done it much better.

471
00:33:28,800 --> 00:33:30,000
It creates like a yes agency.

472
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:32,320
Like take action now, don't just wait.

473
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:44,560
Part of the reason I wanted to push the envelope is that I talk to so many folks many folks here on this podcast and we talked to so many people inside RevenueCat where they have what seems like unreasonably high prices.

474
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:50,960
And my theory now is that, well, one, this is a weather app, it's a commodity good.

475
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:56,800
I should have really better understood like willingness to pay, I should have looked at what other apps are charging and everything.

476
00:33:57,600 --> 00:34:00,640
But the other thing is like the source of attention matters a lot.

477
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:11,680
So the apps that are charging a higher rate, they're either creating a lot of demand through paid advertising where there's not a lot of expectation going into it versus getting press.

478
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:18,320
Like the attention you get, I think makes a difference to that willingness to pay and the type of people you attract.

479
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:21,600
And then especially apps that are really big with search.

480
00:34:21,840 --> 00:34:31,920
If someone is searching for a weather app and is looking for a solution, that's a really different audience than people just seeing it in the press and wanting to check it out.

481
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:36,560
So there's like a different kind of intent, a different kind of user, all those things.

482
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:40,320
And so I think it was a bad move pricing it at 40 bucks.

483
00:34:40,560 --> 00:34:45,040
I think we probably should have done 30 and then done a discount to 1999 for the launch.

484
00:34:45,360 --> 00:34:46,320
Yeah, it's interesting.

485
00:34:46,560 --> 00:34:48,640
Almost definitely, this thought is coming from me.

486
00:34:49,120 --> 00:35:03,520
listening to this podcast, but like the idea that the journey that the user has taken at the point when they see the paywall being super important, which oftentimes is when you're talking about the onboarding experience, but it's also the attribution as well.

487
00:35:04,080 --> 00:35:18,720
It's interesting to think that, yeah, like whether that person came through a splashy launch or press versus the high intent they were searching for it, or they saw an ad on Facebook or whatever, that should also drive what offering you're showing them on that paywall.

488
00:35:18,960 --> 00:35:24,000
I don't know if I've ever seen this before, but it would be interesting to, I guess I have on a launch itself.

489
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:34,640
All the press people that you're trying to get to write an article include a link that has the attribution so that that discount only applies if they get it through their article.

490
00:35:35,280 --> 00:35:42,240
If you're launching an otherwise unknown app with a day of press, you can pretty much guarantee that anyone who buys it on that day is coming from the press, right?

491
00:35:42,480 --> 00:35:54,720
Yeah, but even if 100% of users, that's the case, from the person writing the article story, they being able to say, just tell them that nine to five readers are the only well, that's true.

492
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:55,360
You could just say that.

493
00:35:56,480 --> 00:35:57,680
Am I coming out scrupulous?

494
00:35:59,040 --> 00:35:59,840
Nobody's going to mind.

495
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:00,800
It's literally true.

496
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:02,640
If they click, they'll get the discount.

497
00:36:03,120 --> 00:36:04,640
Maybe everyone else gets the discount too.

498
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:07,040
But I think that's something that could live on though, right?

499
00:36:07,280 --> 00:36:29,840
Like one of the things for me that I do see in the data and also it's in my head that this is a thing is like a lot of these journalists for some of the bigger press junkets, they spend a lot of time on SEO, they make these big listics because they know that one, they'll get a lot of attention on that day, but then they'll get attention for the next six months when someone buys their new iPhone and they're like, Okay, what are these new widgets?

500
00:36:30,080 --> 00:36:32,560
Because that's probably when they actually update their phone at the same time.

501
00:36:32,880 --> 00:36:36,000
That's a driver of traffic over time for your app as well.

502
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:41,760
Each one of these articles that they write, that deal kind of can live there as well.

503
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:46,160
That's also part of that narrative, that user journey all the way to your paywall.

504
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:56,960
So like theoretically, those could even live on there and show a different offering to someone that came from an article versus someone who came from searching, I need to know the weather or whatever in the app store.

505
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:12,960
One other thing I did want to mention on pricing is that I priced based on things that I knew and based on my thinking of the value of Weather Up and my costs in servicing Weather Up, not on the customer pricing.

506
00:37:15,120 --> 00:37:18,880
I think that's a huge thing that founders can get themselves tied up in knots about.

507
00:37:19,040 --> 00:37:26,080
And I succumbed to it myself is that one of the features that we were leading with that I don't think many people are even using.

508
00:37:26,400 --> 00:37:36,720
People didn't even talk about it much, but I was personally so excited about it because this is how I use it is that with WeatherUp 3.0 you can set a different weather source per widget.

509
00:37:37,280 --> 00:37:41,760
And so I have a whole home screen dedicated on my iPhone with three widgets.

510
00:37:42,160 --> 00:37:46,960
And it's Apple Weather as a source, Eris Weather as a source, and AccuWeather as a source.

511
00:37:48,480 --> 00:37:52,560
I can see whether any of the forecasts are showing rain coming.

512
00:37:52,960 --> 00:37:55,520
I can see the differences in the forecast and everything like that.

513
00:37:55,840 --> 00:37:57,360
Super, uber, nerdy feature.

514
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:19,200
Well, it's also very expensive to do that because every 15 minutes or whatever, it's hitting the server not one time to one weather data provider and the weather data is why I had been losing money on the app leading up to this launch was that it's just really expensive to provide weather data and the widgets and complications are especially expensive because they're constantly updating in the background.

515
00:38:19,680 --> 00:38:25,680
So I got myself all twisted in knots thinking, okay, we need to make sure there's a level of profitabilability here.

516
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:35,760
And if a bunch of people start putting five widgets on their home screen with different locations and different sources and everything else, it's going to get really expensive for us to serve these customers.

517
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:44,160
It's really hard to educate customers on how expensive weather data is and then the fact that it's even more expensive because it's like, give me my car.

518
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:44,560
Exactly.

519
00:38:44,640 --> 00:38:45,200
I'm a weather man.

520
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:47,360
Like, I don't tell me about your business model.

521
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:48,240
I don't want to know.

522
00:38:48,720 --> 00:38:55,040
And so I was pricing based on my knowledge of that and my own thinking the feature was going to get super hyped.

523
00:38:55,360 --> 00:38:56,880
And you only have the one tier, right?

524
00:38:57,200 --> 00:38:59,040
It's not like you have a more expensive plan.

525
00:38:59,520 --> 00:39:00,400
I didn't have multiple tier.

526
00:39:01,280 --> 00:39:04,640
So you had to model it in, like, some percentage of users are going to be doing all this.

527
00:39:05,280 --> 00:39:05,360
Yeah.

528
00:39:05,680 --> 00:39:06,000
Exactly.

529
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:06,800
I'll call back.

530
00:39:06,960 --> 00:39:09,360
I don't think we talked about, but you didn't put any product analytics.

531
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:21,760
Two causes of death there were one, you didn't have product analytics to tell you how many users are doing this, which we could probably look at the API calls to see, like, the relative now, you know, the percentages, which then I'm going to bust you for that's another symptom of not launching.

532
00:39:21,840 --> 00:39:27,920
If you had launched this as a standalone thing, you could have, like, not touched anything, just measured how many people are doing what.

533
00:39:28,160 --> 00:39:34,640
And then you would have some idea of, like, okay, actually, as long as we charge X, we're going to be able to cover costs, because not that many people actually use this feature.

534
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:36,000
There's some risk there, but.

535
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:36,320
Yeah.

536
00:39:36,480 --> 00:39:38,560
Yeah, and this is why we talk about all this stuff.

537
00:39:38,720 --> 00:39:40,480
Like, I should have surveyed some users.

538
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:42,160
I should have value based pricing.

539
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:44,720
Value based pricing is typically helpful.

540
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:48,880
I think actually in this case, David, value based pricing is hurtful for your bottom line potentially.

541
00:39:49,120 --> 00:39:53,680
Actually not, because I think if you're closer to the perceived value of the product, you'll get a better optimum.

542
00:39:53,840 --> 00:40:04,480
In value based pricing, you want to set your pricing to the perceived value for some aggregate of users, as opposed to cost based pricing where you're saying it cost me x dollars to deliver, I'm going to charge x plus five percent profit or whatever.

543
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:09,200
In SaaS and stuff, it's really important because the cost to deliver is often very, very low.

544
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:12,160
If you price just to your cost, you're giving away too much.

545
00:40:12,720 --> 00:40:18,640
And often, especially in SaaS where you're like, as a developer, having to ask for $100,000 a year, right?

546
00:40:19,840 --> 00:40:22,160
You really have to be like, gosh, that's so much money for software.

547
00:40:22,320 --> 00:40:29,280
But then you go, well, if I'm replacing a developer or if I'm replacing some amount of headcount or some amount of whatever, now I see the value, right?

548
00:40:29,520 --> 00:40:31,680
The value is there and therefore, you know, you feel it.

549
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:39,040
But you could be on either side of that price curve in the sense that like, you're pricing way above the perceived value, the demand is going to drop off again.

550
00:40:39,200 --> 00:40:41,680
So value-based pricing doesn't necessarily mean charge more.

551
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:48,480
It just means like charge the perceived value because the costs are only something really the provider of the service cares of the company.

552
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:52,480
Well, they're highly variable based on the number of users, which is weird too.

553
00:40:52,640 --> 00:40:52,800
Yeah.

554
00:40:53,120 --> 00:41:03,360
And also like at this stage, like there's some fundamental costs that you can never get below potentially, but like you should really, I mean, when RevenueCat launched, like our cost structures were really brutal in the early days, right?

555
00:41:03,600 --> 00:41:09,600
But it was like, it's okay, because eventually we're going to have a team that's going to focus on making everything efficient and driving down costs and whatever.

556
00:41:09,760 --> 00:41:12,640
But in the early days, it was just like turn up the database dial, right?

557
00:41:12,880 --> 00:41:14,080
Like just keep earning money.

558
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:19,600
Um, but obviously a different thing where you have APIs, you have a more fixed cost.

559
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:22,560
But I think the worst way to do this is to be like, how much did it cost me to build this?

560
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:22,960
Yeah.

561
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:24,240
And then they divide it into that.

562
00:41:24,320 --> 00:41:26,000
It's like, no, don't do that.

563
00:41:26,160 --> 00:41:26,720
Don't do that.

564
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:28,160
That's not going to end well for you.

565
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:44,880
And then the bigger problem for me too was on the value-based pricing front is that people have expectations coming into a weather app, like understanding my market as weather nerd, like who's going to put this widget on their home screen, weather nerd, weather nerd already have five other weather apps.

566
00:41:45,040 --> 00:41:47,040
They're probably subscribed to two other weather apps.

567
00:41:47,440 --> 00:41:50,080
So they have a sense of like what a weather app should cost.

568
00:41:50,480 --> 00:42:03,520
And one of the ones I got compared to which I know Brian, the developer of Carrot Weather, and that's when I got called out against was like, well, Carrot Weather has more features and is only 30$30 a year on their like most premium tier.

569
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:04,640
And it's true.

570
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:12,080
If I had thought more about who I was marketing to, I would have realized that they already had that price comparison in mind.

571
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:18,480
And yeah, it's a real thing to assume the market is already somewhat in equilibrium, especially for something where you have a lot of products.

572
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:22,720
I don't think looking at your competitors, you know, if you're building something truly new, it's harder.

573
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:29,280
But if you're building something that's like, I want to say a commodity, but think of car companies, like car companies are super precise in their pricing, right?

574
00:42:29,520 --> 00:42:31,040
Because most cars are commodities, right?

575
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:32,720
There's like slightly differentiated.

576
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:36,560
And so they have to be laser tight with where the market is and they know they.

577
00:42:36,560 --> 00:42:39,360
And they know they won't sell them if their cost is more than this and stuff like that.

578
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:39,600
Yeah.

579
00:42:40,080 --> 00:42:40,160
Yeah.

580
00:42:40,320 --> 00:42:42,480
And you have to price in that differentiation too, right?

581
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:47,200
I think that was a little bit of where I was hoping that this was differentiated enough and valuable enough.

582
00:42:47,680 --> 00:42:50,080
We got thousands of subscribers at this launch.

583
00:42:50,240 --> 00:42:51,360
It wasn't a complete failure.

584
00:42:51,840 --> 00:43:02,160
So, I mean, I did kind of prove that a certain number of people were willing to pay a premium for what they perceived to be a premium feature and a premium experience.

585
00:43:02,400 --> 00:43:03,600
Now I have the opportunity to experiment.

586
00:43:03,920 --> 00:43:06,000
You had asked earlier about, you know, where do I go from here?

587
00:43:06,240 --> 00:43:07,520
And we kind of already covered it.

588
00:43:07,680 --> 00:43:09,200
It's like, I'm going to launch again.

